29:["$","li","08-page-cache",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#08-page-cache","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"08"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"The page cache: unused RAM is wasted RAM"}]]}]}]
2a:["$","li","09-effective-access-time",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#09-effective-access-time","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"09"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Effective access time, and why engineers care"}]]}]}]
2b:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-the-big-illusion",{"id":"01-the-big-illusion","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The big illusion: more address space than RAM"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Back in the introduction we made a bold promise on the OS's behalf: every process gets a huge, private, contiguous address space that starts at zero and stretches far beyond the physical memory installed in the machine. On a 64-bit system that space is astronomically large, terabytes, and obviously no single program's data is actually that big, nor could it fit in RAM if it were. Virtual memory is how the OS keeps that promise without lying: it decouples the size of the address space from the size of physical memory."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The trick is that a virtual page does not have to live in RAM to exist. A page can be in one of a few states: resident in a physical frame, sitting out on disk in a swap area or backing store, or not yet materialised at all (a freshly allocated but never-touched page). The page table records, for each page, which of these it is. As long as the MMU can find a page or trap to the OS when it cannot, the process experiences one seamless address space, even though its pages are scattered between RAM and disk at any moment."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Virtual memory"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The illusion that each process has a large, private address space independent of how much physical RAM exists; pages live in RAM or on disk as needed."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Resident page"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A virtual page that currently occupies a physical frame in RAM, its page-table entry is valid."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Backing store / swap"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The disk area that holds pages which are not currently in RAM, so the OS can bring them back when touched."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Overcommit"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Allowing the sum of all processes' address spaces to exceed physical RAM, on the bet that not all of it is needed at once."}]]}]]}],"$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}]]}],"$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32","$L33","$L34","$L35","$L36"]}]
2c:["$","$L37",null,{"prev":{"slug":"paging-and-the-mmu","title":"Paging & the MMU","kind":"$undefined","number":22},"next":{"slug":"page-replacement-algorithms","title":"Page Replacement Algorithms","kind":"$undefined","number":24}}]
38:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2d:["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The promise, delivered",": "]}],"A process can address more memory than the machine physically has, because \"addressable\" and \"resident in RAM\" are two different things. Virtual memory is the layer that keeps them separate, and hides the difference from your code."]}]]}]
2e:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F01-the-big-illusion.jpg?v=3850f8c63ab9a8f02734f4c52eecdb19","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a single process's virtual address space drawn as a tall column of pages numbered 0..N; arrows fan out to two destinations, a small \"RAM (frames)\" box holding only a handful of the pages (marked present), and a larger \"disk / swap (backing store)\" box holding the rest (marked not-present). A caption reads \"the process sees the whole column; only the shaded pages are really in RAM right now\". A padlock note reminds that each process gets its own private column."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2f:["$","section","02-demand-paging",{"id":"02-demand-paging","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Demand paging: don't load it until it's touched"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The naive way to run a program is to load the whole thing into memory before it starts. Demand paging does the opposite: load nothing up front, and bring in each page only at the moment the program first refers to it, lazily, on demand. When the OS sets up a process's address space (during exec, say), it maps the pages to their locations on disk but marks every page-table entry as not present by clearing the valid bit. No RAM is committed yet. The very first instruction fetch touches a code page that is not resident, which faults, which pulls that one page in. Execution proceeds page by page, each one arriving just in time."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is the same valid bit from the paging chapter, now doing real work. Then, an invalid entry meant \"not mapped, probably a bug\". Now it means one of two things: either the reference is genuinely illegal (a wild pointer, and the OS delivers a segmentation fault), or the page is legal but simply not in RAM yet (and the OS silently fetches it). The kernel tells them apart by checking the reference against the process's memory map: is this address inside a region the process is allowed to use?"}],["$","ul","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Faster startup: a program begins running after only its first few pages are in, not after the whole binary is read from disk."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Less memory used: pages that a run never touches, error handlers, rarely-used features, a huge lookup table behind a branch you don't take, never occupy a single frame."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Overcommit becomes practical: because a process rarely needs all its pages resident at once, many processes can share limited RAM."}]]}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Lazy on purpose",": "]}],"Demand paging is laziness as an optimisation. The cheapest page to load is the one you never touch, so the OS refuses to load any page until the program proves it needs it by faulting on it."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":["$L39","$L3a"]}]]}]]}]
30:["$","section","03-the-page-fault",{"id":"03-the-page-fault","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The page fault, step by step"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The page fault is the single most important mechanism in this chapter, and it is worth being able to recite. A page fault is not an error in the everyday sense, it is a hardware trap that says \"the page you want is not present; someone in the kernel needs to deal with this before the instruction can complete\". Here is the full round trip."}],["$","ul","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The CPU executes an instruction that references a virtual address; the MMU walks the page table to translate it."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The MMU finds the page-table entry with its valid bit clear. It cannot complete the translation, so it raises a page-fault trap, the CPU switches to kernel mode and jumps to the page-fault handler, saving enough state to resume later."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The handler first decides whether the reference is even legal by checking the faulting address against the process's memory regions. If it is bogus, the process gets a SIGSEGV. If it is legal, this is a real demand-paging fault and the handler continues."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The OS locates the page's contents on disk, in the swap area, or in the file that backs this mapping, and picks a physical frame to hold it. If no frame is free, it must evict a resident page to make room (that eviction decision is the whole of the next chapter)."}]]}],["$","li","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"The OS issues a disk read to copy the page into the chosen frame. Because disk is slow, the faulting process is blocked and the CPU is handed to some other ready process in the meantime, a page fault is a scheduling event as much as a memory event."}]]}],["$","li","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"When the read completes, the OS updates the page-table entry to point at the frame and sets its valid bit, then returns from the trap."}]]}],["$","li","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":["$L3b","$L3c"]}]]}],"$L3d","$L3e"]}],"$L3f"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","04-minor-vs-major-faults",{"id":"04-minor-vs-major-faults","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Minor vs major faults"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Not all page faults cost the same, and the difference is enormous. The OS and hardware both distinguish two kinds. A minor fault (also called a soft fault) happens when the page is already somewhere in physical memory, it just is not yet mapped into this process's page table. Maybe another process already loaded the same file page, maybe it is sitting in the page cache, maybe it is a copy-on-write page being shared. The handler only has to point a page-table entry at an existing frame and set the valid bit. No disk involved; the whole thing takes microseconds or less."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A major fault (a hard fault) is the expensive one: the page is not in RAM at all and must be read from disk, from the swap area or from a file. Now the process blocks on physical I/O, and even on a fast SSD that is orders of magnitude slower than a memory access; on a spinning disk it is worse still. The ratio between a memory access and a disk read is roughly a thousandfold or more, so a program's performance is dominated not by how many faults it takes but by how many of them are major."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is not academic, you can measure it directly, and doing so is often the fastest way to diagnose a program that is mysteriously slow or that gets slower as memory pressure rises."}],["$","figure","3",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"GNU time reports both fault counts; watch the ratio, not just the totals."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ /usr/bin/time -v ./my_program\n   ...\n   Maximum resident set size (kbytes): 812304\n   Major (requiring I/O) page faults: 12\n   Minor (reclaiming a frame) page faults: 40213\n   ...\n\n# A live view across all processes: the \"maj/min flt\" columns.\n$ ps -o pid,maj_flt,min_flt,cmd -p $(pgrep -n my_program)\n  PID MAJFL   MINFL CMD\n 4812   12   40213 ./my_program"}]}]]}],["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The number that matters",": "]}],"Tens of thousands of minor faults are usually harmless, that is just memory being wired up on demand. A steadily climbing major-fault count is the alarm bell: your working set no longer fits in RAM and the machine is going to disk to make up the difference."]}]]}]]}],"$L40"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","05-locality-working-set",{"id":"05-locality-working-set","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Locality and the working set: why this works at all"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Demand paging sounds like it should be catastrophically slow, surely a program constantly hitting pages that are not resident will spend all its time waiting on disk? It does not, and the reason is one of the most important empirical facts in computing: locality of reference. Programs do not touch their memory uniformly at random. Over any short window they touch a small, clustered set of pages, again and again."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Temporal locality"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A page touched recently is likely to be touched again soon, loop bodies, hot data structures, the current stack frame."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Spatial locality"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"If a page is touched, nearby pages are likely to be touched too, sequential array scans, adjacent struct fields, instruction streams."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Working set"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The set of pages a process has referenced in the recent past (a sliding time window); a good estimate of what it needs resident right now."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The working-set model turns locality into a strategy. If the OS keeps each process's working set resident in RAM, then the overwhelming majority of memory references hit pages that are already present, so faults are rare, and the ones that occur are cheap minor faults. Demand paging pays its startup cost once, warms up the working set, and then runs at near-full memory speed. This is precisely why loading a whole program up front is wasteful: most of it is never in the working set at any given moment."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The model also predicts exactly when things fall apart. If the combined working sets of the running processes exceed physical RAM, the OS cannot keep them all resident. Pages the processes actively need keep getting evicted and immediately faulted back in, and major faults explode. The system spends its time shuttling pages to and from disk instead of doing work, thrashing, which we devote a later chapter to. The working set is the boundary between \"virtual memory is free money\" and \"virtual memory is killing us\"."}],"$L41"]}],"$L42"]}]]}]
33:["$","section","06-copy-on-write",{"id":"06-copy-on-write","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Copy-on-write: making fork() cheap"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Back in the process chapters we met fork(), which creates a child process as a duplicate of its parent, same code, same data, same open files. Taken literally, that means copying the parent's entire address space, which for a large process could be gigabytes. And it is usually wasted work, because the very next thing the child does is often exec() a different program, throwing all those copied pages away. Copy-on-write (COW) is the optimisation that makes fork() cheap, and it is built entirely from the page-fault machinery."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When a process forks, the OS does not copy any data pages. Instead it gives the child its own page table whose entries point at the parent's existing frames, and marks every shared page read-only in both processes, tagging it internally as copy-on-write. As long as both processes only read, they happily share the same physical frames, and fork has cost almost nothing but a page table. The copy is deferred until it is actually needed."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The moment either process tries to write to a COW page, the read-only mapping causes a protection fault, a trap into the kernel. The handler recognises it as a COW fault, allocates a fresh frame, copies the one page into it, points the writing process's page-table entry at the private copy, and marks both copies writable again. The instruction restarts and the write succeeds. Only the pages actually written ever get duplicated; everything else stays shared for the life of the processes. This is why fork() followed by exec() is fast, and why forking a huge process is not the disaster it looks like."}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Same trap, new job",": "]}],"COW is the page fault wearing a different hat. Instead of \"fetch this page from disk\", the fault means \"you may not write to this shared page, let me give you your own copy first\". The valid/protection bits turn one mechanism into many features."]}]]}]]}],["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F06-copy-on-write.jpg?v=c6c2b2cb096b04ae5162f6f2fa548563","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: fork() drawn in three panels. Panel 1: parent process with its pages in RAM. Panel 2: after fork(), child gets its own page table but every entry points at the SAME parent frames, all marked read-only and flagged COW, no data copied, big label \"instant, no copy\". Panel 3: the child writes to one page → the read-only access traps → OS copies just that one frame, points the child's PTE at the copy and makes both writable → arrow labelled \"the write fault triggers the copy\". A note: shared code/unwritten pages stay shared forever."}],"$L43"]}]]}]]}]
34:["$","section","07-memory-mapped-files",{"id":"07-memory-mapped-files","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Memory-mapped files: mmap"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Demand paging does not only pull pages from swap, it can pull them straight from a file, and that is what memory-mapped files expose to you directly. The mmap() system call maps a file (or part of one) into a range of your virtual address space. After that, you access the file's contents as ordinary memory: dereference a pointer to read a byte, assign through a pointer to write one. There is no read() or write() in the loop at all."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Under the hood it is pure demand paging. mmap sets up the mapping with every page marked not-present; it copies nothing. The first time you touch a page of the mapping, you take a page fault, and the OS reads the corresponding block of the file into a frame and maps it in, exactly the fault flow from earlier, with the file as the backing store instead of swap. Pages you never touch are never read. Two processes that map the same file can share the same physical frames, which is precisely how the OS loads shared libraries once and shares them across every program that uses them."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Map a file and read it as memory, the kernel faults the pages in for you."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"c"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"#include <sys/mman.h>\n#include <sys/stat.h>\n#include <fcntl.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n\nint main(int argc, char **argv) {\n    int fd = open(argv[1], O_RDONLY);\n    struct stat st; fstat(fd, &st);\n\n    /* Project the whole file into our address space. No data read yet. */\n    char *data = mmap(NULL, st.st_size, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);\n    if (data == MAP_FAILED) { perror(\"mmap\"); return 1; }\n\n    /* Touching data[i] faults the matching file page in on demand. */\n    long spaces = 0;\n    for (off_t i = 0; i < st.st_size; i++)\n        if (data[i] == ' ') spaces++;\n\n    printf(\"%ld spaces\\n\", spaces);\n    munmap(data, st.st_size);\n    close(fd);\n    return 0;\n}"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is not a toy. Program loaders map executables and shared libraries this way; language runtimes map large read-only data files; and databases lean on mmap (or the same underlying page-cache machinery) to treat on-disk files as if they were memory, letting the OS handle which parts are resident. It lets you work with a file far larger than RAM using nothing but pointers, and it avoids the extra copy that read() makes into your buffer, the file page in the cache is the memory you read."}],"$L44"]}],"$L45"]}]]}]
35:["$","section","08-page-cache",{"id":"08-page-cache","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"The page cache: unused RAM is wasted RAM"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Once you see file data flowing through frames on demand, a bigger picture snaps into focus. The OS does not let file pages evaporate after you use them, it keeps them in RAM in the page cache, so the next access to the same file data is served from memory with no disk I/O at all. Every read() and write(), not just mmap, goes through this cache. It is why the second time you grep a file it is instant, and why a freshly booted machine feels sluggish until the caches warm."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is where the famous slogan comes from: unused RAM is wasted RAM. If you look at a healthy Linux box, almost none of its memory is \"free\", the OS has filled the otherwise-idle RAM with cached file pages, because a cached page might save a disk read and an empty frame can never save anything. The page cache is not memory being hogged; it is memory being put to work, and the OS will hand any of it back the instant a process actually needs the frame. The page cache and process memory draw from the same physical pool, which is exactly why the memory picture blurs: the same demand-paging machinery, the same frames, the same eviction decisions serve both."}],["$","figure","2",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"On Linux, most \"used\" RAM is reclaimable cache, the buff/cache column, not lost memory."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"$$ free -h\n              total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\nMem:           15Gi       4.2Gi       0.4Gi       0.3Gi        11Gi        10Gi\n#                                    ^ tiny         ^ page cache     ^ what a new\n#                                    \"free\"         doing real work   process could get"}]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"For engineers this has a sharp consequence, and it is where this whole course is heading. A database keeps its own buffer pool, an in-process cache of disk pages, sitting on top of the OS page cache. The same file block can end up cached twice: once by Postgres in its shared buffers, once by the kernel in its page cache. That double-caching, and the tug-of-war over who should manage memory, is a recurring theme when you tune a database. We will pick it apart in the capstone; for now, just register that the OS-level virtual-memory machinery you have learned is the foundation the database's own memory management is built on top of."}],"$L46"]}],"$L47"]}]]}]
36:["$","section","09-effective-access-time",{"id":"09-effective-access-time","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"09"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Effective access time, and why engineers care"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Everything in this chapter comes down to one uncomfortable number, and it is worth working it out. Call the probability that a given memory access causes a page fault p, the page-fault rate. A normal access costs a memory-access time; a fault costs that plus the whole fault-service time, which is dominated by the disk read. The average, or effective access time, is a simple weighted sum."}],["$","figure","1",{"className":"overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-ink/15 bg-ink/[0.04]","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex items-center justify-between border-b border-ink/10 bg-ink/[0.03] px-3.5 py-1.5","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-wide text-mute","children":"Effective access time, worked through with realistic numbers."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"font-mono text-[0.7rem] text-accent/70","children":"bash"}]]}],["$","pre",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3 text-[0.86rem] leading-[1.6]","children":["$","code",null,{"className":"font-mono text-ink/90","children":"EAT = (1 - p) * memory_access_time  +  p * page_fault_time\n\nmemory_access_time =        100 ns\npage_fault_time    =        8 ms = 8,000,000 ns   (a disk read dominates)\n\np = 1/1000        EAT = 0.999*100 + 0.001*8,000,000   = ~8100 ns   (~81x slower)\np = 1/100,000     EAT = 100 + 8,000,000/100,000       = ~180 ns    (~1.8x slower)\np = 1/10,000,000  EAT = 100 + 8,000,000/10,000,000    = ~100.8 ns  (<1% slower)"}]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Stare at those numbers. A fault every thousand accesses, which sounds rare, makes memory run roughly eighty times slower on average, because a single 8-millisecond disk read is worth eighty thousand 100-nanosecond memory accesses. To keep the slowdown under 10%, the fault rate has to fall below about one in 800,000. That is the tyranny of the disk gap: the fault penalty is so enormous that only an extremely low major-fault rate is tolerable. This is the quantitative reason locality matters so much, it is what drives p down to the tiny values that make virtual memory usable."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This calculation also tells you what failure looks like. Push memory demand past what RAM can hold and p climbs; EAT climbs with it; the system slows to a crawl while the disk light stays on solid. That is thrashing, the subject of a coming chapter. And when the OS cannot satisfy demand even by paging, when there is genuinely not enough memory plus swap, it stops being polite and invokes the OOM killer, terminating a process outright to reclaim its frames. Every backend engineer who has seen a container get \"OOM-killed\" under load has met the far end of this curve."}],["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":["$L48","$L49","$L4a","$L4b"]}],"$L4c"]}],"$L4d"]}]]}]
39:["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F02-demand-paging.jpg?v=c1eb2c15841768a73d41dcc4158359e2","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a program binary on disk split into pages (code, data, big rarely-used table). A timeline shows exec() creating the address space with every page-table entry marked \"not present\" (valid bit = 0). Below, three separate memory accesses each pull in exactly one page on first touch, code page, then a data page, while the giant unused table stays on disk, greyed out and labelled \"never loaded\"."}]
3a:["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]
3b:["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}]
3c:["$","span",null,{"children":"Crucially, the CPU restarts the very instruction that faulted. This time the translation succeeds, the access completes, and the program continues with no idea anything unusual happened."}]
3d:["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That final restart is the subtle, elegant part. The faulting instruction is not skipped or emulated, it is simply re-run from scratch now that its page is present. This is why the whole scheme is invisible to your program: from its point of view the memory access just took a while. It is also why instructions must be restartable, a constraint that shapes CPU design."}]
3e:["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Narrate it cold",": "]}],"Not present → trap → legal? → find on disk → get a frame (maybe evict) → read it in → fix the PTE → restart the instruction. If you can say that without notes, you understand demand paging."]}]]}]
3f:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F03-the-page-fault.jpg?v=15bf26ca844b7328820a6664b6c64ea6","alt":"Handwritten A4 flow crossing the user/kernel line: (1) CPU executes an instruction referencing virtual page P; (2) MMU walks the page table, sees valid bit = 0, raises a page-fault trap; (3) kernel page-fault handler checks the address is legal in the process map; (4) OS finds P on disk (swap/file), picks a free frame F (or evicts one, arrow to \"ch24\"); (5) disk read copies P into F, process meanwhile blocked; (6) OS updates the PTE to point at F and sets valid = 1; (7) return-from-trap RESTARTS the same instruction, which now succeeds. A big label reads \"the instruction runs as if nothing happened\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
40:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F04-minor-vs-major-faults.jpg?v=342b18021ac97a4ed67ccbcee933fab4","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two branches from a page-fault trap. Left branch \"MINOR fault, no disk\": the page is already in a physical frame (in the page cache, or shared by another process, or a COW page) and the OS just adds a PTE pointing at it, a short curved arrow, labelled \"~microsecond\". Right branch \"MAJOR fault, hits disk\": the page must be read from swap or a file, a long arrow down to a disk platter, labelled \"~milliseconds, ~1000x slower\". Below, a mock /usr/bin/time -v output boxes \"Major (requiring I/O) page faults: 12\" and \"Minor (reclaiming a frame) page faults: 40213\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
41:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The whole bet",": "]}],"Demand paging is a bet that your working set is much smaller than your address space and fits in RAM. For almost all real programs that bet pays off, which is why we can pretend memory is nearly infinite and mostly get away with it."]}]]}]
42:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F05-locality-working-set.jpg?v=928daa422faf23810459de37d0b8718c","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a horizontal timeline of page references (a stream of page numbers). A sliding window of the last few references is shaded and labelled \"working set = pages touched in this window\". Above, two clusters show temporal locality (same page hit again and again) and spatial locality (neighbouring pages hit in sequence). A side note: \"keep the working set resident → almost all faults are minor → speed. Working set > RAM → major faults explode → thrashing (ch25)\". A small graph plots fault rate low while working set fits, then spiking sharply past the RAM line."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
43:["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]
44:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Files as memory",": "]}],"mmap dissolves the line between \"file\" and \"memory\". You stop issuing I/O calls and start touching memory; the page-fault handler becomes your I/O layer, loading exactly the pages you use, exactly when you use them."]}]]}]
45:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F07-memory-mapped-files.jpg?v=9ef61e0538efd8f6d8bbb005f63dac83","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a file on disk drawn as a row of blocks. An mmap() call projects that file directly into a region of the process's virtual address space (a matching row of pages), page-for-page, all initially not-present. The program reads/writes memory with plain pointers; each first touch triggers a demand-paging fault that pulls the matching file block into a frame. A second process mmap-ing the same file is shown sharing the same frames. Side note contrasts \"mmap: touch memory, kernel does the I/O\" vs \"read()/write(): explicit syscalls + a copy into your buffer\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
46:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["DB angle",": "]}],"When you set a database's buffer-pool size, you are negotiating with the OS page cache over the same physical RAM. Understanding demand paging and the page cache is what lets you reason about that trade instead of guessing."]}]]}]
47:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F08-page-cache.jpg?v=2b1e9962ac07786ec75b9f2cd2a48de7","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a RAM bar split into three zones: \"process pages\", \"page cache (file data)\", and a thin \"truly free\" sliver. An arrow shows a file read() populating the page cache; a second read of the same file is served straight from cache (label \"no disk!\"). Below, two stacked layers for a database: the OS page cache sitting under the DB's own buffer pool, with a note \"same file data can sit in both, double caching\" pointing forward to the capstone. A caption: \"free RAM is a wasted resource, the OS fills it with cached file pages and evicts them the instant a process needs the memory\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
48:["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Effective access time (EAT)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The average cost of a memory access once you weight in the rare-but-huge cost of page faults."}]]}]
49:["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Page-fault rate (p)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The fraction of memory accesses that fault; because the fault penalty is ~a thousandfold, p must be tiny to keep EAT near RAM speed."}]]}]
4a:["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Thrashing"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The runaway state where working sets exceed RAM, p spikes, and the machine spends its time paging instead of computing (next-but-one chapter)."}]]}]
4b:["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"OOM killer"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The OS's last resort when memory truly runs out: kill a process to reclaim frames rather than page forever."}]]}]
4c:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Where this hands off",": "]}],"We now know why RAM is precious and why faults must stay rare. The unanswered question is the one buried in step 4 of the page fault: when a page must come in and no frame is free, which resident page do we evict? Choose badly and you cause the next fault yourself. That decision, page replacement, is the whole of the next chapter."]}]]}]
4d:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L38",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch23%2F09-effective-access-time.jpg?v=65ce77bc9a05f86e466518d7032f0fc6","alt":"Handwritten A4 worksheet: at top the formula EAT = (1 - p) * memory_time + p * fault_time. Below, plugged-in numbers: memory access = 100 ns, page-fault service = 8 ms = 8,000,000 ns. A small table of fault rates p = 1/1000, 1/100000, 1/10000000 with the resulting EAT (roughly 8100 ns, 180 ns, 100.8 ns) and a slowdown factor beside each. A bold takeaway box: \"to stay within 10% of RAM speed you need fewer than 1 fault per ~800,000 accesses\". An arrow points forward to \"thrashing (ch25)\" and a small tombstone labelled \"OOM killer\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
